import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,an as s}from"./chunks/framework.CYOK54rb.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why use GoMeta?","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/why-use-gometa.md","filePath":"concepts/why-use-gometa.md"}'),n={name:"concepts/why-use-gometa.md"};function r(i,e,l,c,h,m){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="Why-use-GoMeta?" tabindex="-1">Why use <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>? <a class="header-anchor" href="#Why-use-GoMeta?" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why use GoMeta? {#Why-use-GoMeta?}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>&#39;s creator explains <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> by asking and answering three questions. This page answers the second. Why would someone want to use <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>? Among the answers are the four use cases and <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>&#39;s reach: it can be used within all kinds of comments, across languages and file types. The four use cases come first, in the creator&#39;s own words from his JuliaCon 2026 proposal; then why the metadata lives in comments; then the reach beyond programmers — and to AI agents.</p><h2 id="1.-Four-use-cases" tabindex="-1">1. Four use cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#1.-Four-use-cases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Four use cases {#1.-Four-use-cases}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the words of <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>&#39;s creator — his proposal, once it has said that <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> absorbs the metadata and that the result can then be passed to a separate program: &quot;At this point the question may arise: What would such a program or plug-in possibly use this for? What can actually be gained from embedding formalized metadata within comments?&quot; — &quot;To address this question, before continuing with the technicalities of <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>, lets consider four, seemingly unrelated use cases which together ought to resonate with the vast majority of JuliaCon&#39;s attendees:&quot;</p><p><strong>Find it again.</strong> &quot;Over time a large number of Julia files can accumulate. Wouldn&#39;t it be wonderful to speedily retrieve any desired, long forgotten poignant example, powerful code snippet, insightful explanatory statement or laboriously tweaked plot related to some task at hand? Adding meaning to content can change the game. On top of that, an instrument solving this problem might prove even more invaluable when collaborating in a group sharing a repository of documents and it is also not restricted to Julia files only.&quot;</p><p><strong>Link and swap.</strong> &quot;One might want to [inter]link a certain fragment of one file with another fragment, possibly contained in some other file. E.g.: linking an intricate piece of code to an enlightening explanation or a clarifying toy example. Occasionally it could also be handy to swap entire sections of text / code of one file with alternate versions, depending on the circumstances.&quot;</p><p><strong>One source, many outputs.</strong> &quot;Would you like to harness the power of Documenter, enjoying simplified control while sticking to pure Julia files rather than having to deal with additional markdown documents? More generally, a Julia file can serve as a source for all kinds of outputs.&quot;</p><p><strong>Share on your terms.</strong> &quot;Being able to control how individual blocks of code / text are being executed, respectively rendered, can be beneficial. E.g.: certain passages of a file might be intended for personal usage only. Marking them as such would allow automated removal before sharing. <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>&#39;s own source code, available on GitHub, has been processed this way. Another example is the ability to restrict execution to code blocks pertaining to a particular group only.&quot;</p><p>The use cases illustrate what <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> can be used for — but they are not <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>: the core of <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> is not meant to materialize them but to enable <a href="/reference/syntax-and-semantics#the-GoMeta-Engine,-Extension">Extensions</a> to do so — Core Extensions, which are to ship with <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> by default, and additional Extensions, which can be added (e.g. via modules, etc.). <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> evaluates the metadata; an Extension acts on the evaluations — a renderer, a program that writes a notebook or documentation, a program of your own. What ships with this release for each of the four: a demo program under the package&#39;s <code><span class="gm-nb">extensions/</span></code> directory answers, for the package&#39;s committed examples and their five generic labels, which pieces of which files carry a given <a href="/reference/syntax-and-semantics#label">label</a>; the web of atomic data units — the second use case grown large — requires not only an Extension but also a database as a back end, and that Extension does not ship yet; one marked source file in the package derives one notebook and its executed twin, and two pages of the package&#39;s own manual; and the basic renderer, a development tool, writes a copy of a file with its hidden parts commented out and its discarded parts left out. Strictly speaking the basic renderer is no Extension, though it behaves like one: it lives under the package&#39;s development tools, and it is there because it serves a vital purpose for the package&#39;s development. The <a href="./../examples/">Examples</a> page shows the committed files; the <a href="./extensions">Extensions</a> page says what an Extension is and receives.</p><h2 id="2.-Why-in-comments?" tabindex="-1">2. Why in comments? <a class="header-anchor" href="#2.-Why-in-comments?" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Why in comments? {#2.-Why-in-comments?}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The creator&#39;s answers, from his talk&#39;s slides and his proposal:</p><ul><li><p><strong>One source file, all kinds of output formats</strong> — his list of output formats: &quot;HTML - pdf - Jupyter notebook - Documenter markdown - Standard markdown - Clean Julia script - . . .&quot; In his words: &quot;Similar to Literate &amp; Co., segments of code and text can live happily together, weaved into the same files. These serve as source files which can subsequently be converted into a variety of formats such as notebooks, standard markdown or even documenter markdown files. A suitable system for metadata allows exerting precise control over these format&#39;s wide ranging capabilities from within the Julia source file and provides the means to generalize Literate&#39;s core idea and more.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>The same metadata everywhere</strong> — &quot;Julia - Python - C - Word / LaTeX - etc. etc.&quot; — &quot;Language agnostic since metadata remains &quot;hidden&quot; within the comments.&quot; The host file stays an ordinary file of its format: the host language sees nothing but a comment, and the <a href="/reference/syntax-and-semantics#metaLine">metaLines</a> travel with the content they describe.</p></li><li><p><strong>Webs instead of hierarchies</strong> — &quot;Put things into context by weaving … webs of atomic data units … analogous to our brain.&quot; — &quot;Much more capable of representing reality than hierarchies are.&quot; A folder tree or a chapter outline places each item at one position; labels weave a web, and one piece of content carries as many labels as apply.</p></li><li><p><strong>A wider tool</strong> — for all kinds of work (e.g. code, reports, teaching material, note keeping, group projects, etc.) — &quot;Extends well beyond a programming tool&quot;. In the creator&#39;s words: &quot;The concept extends well beyond Julia and can be applied not only to other programming languages but to all sorts of text documents such as lectures notes or any number of scripts concerning a project, research or otherwise.&quot;</p></li></ul><h2 id="3.-Not-just-for-programmers-—-and-for-AI-agents-too" tabindex="-1">3. Not just for programmers — and for AI agents too <a class="header-anchor" href="#3.-Not-just-for-programmers-—-and-for-AI-agents-too" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Not just for programmers — and for AI agents too {#3.-Not-just-for-programmers-—-and-for-AI-agents-too}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At this release <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> reads three <a href="/reference/syntax-and-semantics#comment-marker">comment markers</a> — Julia&#39;s <code>#</code>, the C family&#39;s <code><span class="gm-nb">//</span></code> (e.g. C, Rust, etc.), LaTeX&#39;s <code>%</code> — and it is meant for all kinds of comments, documents and files; <a href="./what-is-gometa">What is <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span>?</a> lists the carriers the creator&#39;s talk names and their state. In his words: &quot;This is not just for programmers any more.&quot;</p><p>The creator&#39;s aims, and a forecast of his talk, reach further. On the webs: one of the main aims of <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> is to interlink documents and files — more generally, content contained in different documents or files — so that, for instance, a paragraph in one document can refer to a sub-section of another; the aim is to create webs of atomic data units, useful not only for humans but also for AI agents. And from his talk, on the labeling itself: &quot;In the near future, AI will automate much of the labeling in the background. Crucially, if this black box is confined within a formalized framework of metadata, intermediary steps can be systematically recorded, making the process not only better traceable but allowing for subsequent adjustments, if necessary – potentially enabling the agent to improve over time.&quot;</p><p>From here: <a href="./how-does-gometa-work">How does <span class="gm-name">GoMeta</span> work?</a> — one run, from a file to what the Extensions receive; <a href="./../tutorials/getting-started">Getting started</a> — the install and one complete run; <a href="./../examples/">Examples</a> — the committed files.</p>',16)])])}const p=a(n,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
